WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.911
API error contracts should be consistent and intentionally limited.

2
00:00:03.911 --> 00:00:10.275
Clients benefit from stable response shapes, safe error codes, appropriate
statuses, and request identifiers.

3
00:00:10.275 --> 00:00:18.759
They do not need raw Python exception objects, database driver messages,
framework tracebacks, internal hostnames, or third-party response bodies.

4
00:00:18.759 --> 00:00:26.913
A safe API boundary translates internal failure into application-level
meaning while preserving detailed diagnostics in controlled systems.

5
00:00:26.913 --> 00:00:32.945
Background jobs and queues need special attention because failure may not
have an immediate user response.

6
00:00:32.945 --> 00:00:39.176
A worker might process a message, call a database, contact a third-party
API, write a file, and retry on failure.

7
00:00:39.176 --> 00:00:46.600
Its exceptions can end up in queue metadata, retry dashboards, logs, dead-
letter stores, alert payloads, and operational tickets.

8
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:54.489
Those destinations should not receive secrets or unredacted downstream
content simply because the failure happened outside a web request.

9
00:00:54.489 --> 00:00:58.731
Third-party and downstream failures should not be passed through blindly.

10
00:00:58.731 --> 00:01:08.674
A payment provider, identity service, database driver, object storage API,
webhook endpoint, or internal service may return messages meant for
developers or administrators.

11
00:01:08.674 --> 00:01:16.165
The Python application should translate those details into safe client
responses and log only the context needed for investigation.

12
00:01:16.165 --> 00:01:22.263
This reduces leakage and keeps dependency-specific behavior from becoming
part of the public API contract.

13
00:01:22.263 --> 00:01:26.307
Retryable and non-retryable errors should be separated at a high level.

14
00:01:26.307 --> 00:01:35.521
Timeouts, rate limits, temporary network failures, validation errors,
authorization failures, and permanent business conflicts require different
responses.

15
00:01:35.521 --> 00:01:44.204
If exception handling treats them all the same, it may retry unsafe work,
hide abuse signals, leak downstream detail, or create confusing client
behavior.

16
00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:49.640
Good failure design makes dependency problems understandable internally
and safe externally.
